content.theory:
Before ES6, JavaScript built objects with constructor functions and `prototype`. The `class` syntax (ES6) gives you the same behavior with a cleaner, more familiar shape. A class is a **blueprint** for objects: define it once, then create many instances with `new`.

### Basic class syntax
```js
class Animal {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }
  speak() {
    return this.name + " makes a sound";
  }
}

const dog = new Animal("Rex");
console.log(dog.speak());   // Rex makes a sound
```
The `constructor` runs once when you call `new Animal(...)` and sets up the instance. Methods live on the class and are shared by every instance.

### Getters and setters
```js
class Circle {
  constructor(radius) {
    this.radius = radius;
  }
  get area() {
    return Math.PI * this.radius * this.radius;
  }
}
console.log(new Circle(2).area);   // accessed like a property, no () call
```
`get` exposes computed values as plain properties — call `circle.area`, not `circle.area()`.

### Inheritance with extends and super
```js
class Dog extends Animal {
  constructor(name, breed) {
    super(name);        // call the parent constructor first
    this.breed = breed;
  }
  speak() {
    return this.name + " barks";
  }
}
```
`extends` makes `Dog` inherit everything from `Animal`. `super(name)` calls the parent constructor — and you MUST call it before using `this` in a subclass. Overriding `speak()` lets the child replace the parent's behavior.

### Static methods
```js
class MathUtils {
  static add(a, b) {
    return a + b;
  }
}
console.log(MathUtils.add(2, 3));   // 5
```
`static` methods belong to the class itself, not to instances — call them on `MathUtils`, not on an instance.

A common mistake: forgetting `super()` in a subclass constructor, which throws a ReferenceError. Another: treating classes like those in Java or C# — JavaScript classes are still prototype-based under the hood, just with sugar on top. Remember `new` before the class name; calling `Animal("Rex")` without `new` throws a TypeError.

